<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasishthiputra Pulumavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Pulumavi II, Vasishthiputra Sri Pulamavi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasishthiputra Pulumavi was a Satavahana king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in the second century CE, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a>. Under him the kingdom extended from the western coast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> delta, and his inscriptions appear at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, Karle and Amaravati. The geographer Ptolemy names a king Siri Polemaios, usually identified with him, as ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Paithan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasishthiputra-pulumavi.jpg" alt="Vasishthiputra Pulumavi" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satavahanas._Sri_Vasisthiputra_Pulumavi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
